    "body": "As a moderator for family videoconferencing with 20+ participants who are mostly tech-illiterate, and who need to be muted when they're not talking because they make crazy noises or have bad microphones, I've found the biggest usability problem to be that they don't know how to unmute themselves. EVEN IF YOU TELL THEM, and say \"You are muted, uncle! Move the mouse and click the unmute button at the bottom of the screen!\" and they spend 5 minutes looking for it (I wish I was exaggerating).\r\n\r\nI understand that a participant can't directly unmute another for privacy reasons (I presume), but what would help tremendously here is the ability to _help_ a participant unmute themselves, by having a \"Ask participant to unmute\" action in the context menu, which would then show a big fat OBVIOUS centered overlay prompt on the participant's screen that says:\r\n\r\n> $other_participant_name_if_set is asking you to unmute so they can hear you. Would you like to unmute now? [Unmute] [Stay muted]\r\n\r\nIf the request comes from someone who has not set their nickname, then obviously the message would be slightly different:\r\n\r\n> Another participant is asking you to unmute so they can hear you. Would you like to unmute now? [Unmute] [Stay muted]\r\n\r\nAnd make those \"Unmute\" and \"Stay muted\" action buttons BIG, OBVIOUS COLORFUL BUTTONS, not dark-blue hyperlinks (which are tiny and hard to see due to the dark background behind them).\r\n\r\nChoosing \"Stay muted\" would probably dismiss the prompt for a minimum of 20 seconds.",
